(project tuna
 (doc (|
      Tuna is a standalone evaluator for tree calculus (Barry Jay /
      Johannes Bader): a store-backed daemon where the unit of
      execution is a content-hash-addressed tree, executed by five
      rewrite rules under a fuel and size budget, with all effects at
      a journaled, capability-gated boundary.

      This book specs v1 — the effects release: capability grants,
      the effect journal, replay verification, and path-tagged call
      sites. The v0 handoff spec (interpreter, store, hash/patch/run
      API, REPL, differential harness) lives in ./SPEC.md, pulled from
      the pricklypear note spec_tuna; when tuna gets code, SPEC.md is
      replaced by chapters here and v0's acceptance criteria move into
      borg/acceptance.borg.

      Framing: tuna is an academic project. The research question is
      "does the structural seam pay for itself — programs as trees
      addressed by hash, effects as journaled boundary events?" The
      expected findings are failures, and borg/acceptance.borg defines
      failure in advance so the project cannot limp forever in
      ambiguous half-working state.

      v1 thesis, stated so it can be refuted: in this design the
      journal IS the system. The pure core is derivable — a run's pure
      part can always be re-executed — while the journal is the only
      non-reproducible artifact, the recorded environment. Replay is
      not a debugging feature bolted on after the fact; it is a
      consistency proof between those two halves.

      RUNTIME PURITY THESIS (operator, 2026-09-15 — spec-level stance,
      binds all future code): tunacore admits no scheduler. The
      evaluation core's end state is a PURE STEP — a value->value
      state transition, no monad parameter, no scheduler coupling, no
      Lwt/async types anywhere under common/, interpreter/, compiler/.
      Concretely: `Prim_eval.Make(Lwt)` in server/lib is a MIGRATION
      stage, not the end state. In an impure language like OCaml a
      monad in the core is either ceremony (identity) or a concealed
      scheduler (a promise runtime) — both are excluded here; the
      non-negotiable invariants (verbatim triage rules, exact,
      strategy-ordered step counts, fuel/size exhaustion as values)
      are what the M3 corpus referees, and they must survive any core
      re-shape bit-for-bit. The host, and only the host, owns the
      runtime: its loop may bind Lwt, eio, or OCaml 5 effects — run
      suspension/stepping (journal.borg, replay) is a GOAL the pure
      step function makes natural, and the M10+ effects experiment
      (see .ralph/plan.md Open Questions) is the designated bridge.
      Failure definition: if a core re-shape cannot reproduce corpus
      step counts identical to the verbatim-port reference, that is a
      spec violation (corpus red), not a design variant; if a real
      workload exposes an F-class failure of the thesis itself (the
      journal boundary cannot drive a pure-step core without leaking
      scheduler state back into the core), record it in FINDINGS.md
      under the acceptance.borg failure regime with evidence.
    |))
 (status planned)
 (* agent note (|2026-09-15 chapter created. Provenance: pp-eval against pricklypear.rocks pulled note "Tuna: standalone tree evaluator - spec v0" (slug spec_tuna) into SPEC.md. Design discussion before authoring settled: (1) seam-of-structure vs seam-of-text is the honest frame — PP's /api/eval parses hostile strings, tuna's eval path has no parser; (2) "everything else becomes a side effect" inverts into "the journal is the recorded environment = replay sandbox, replay needs nothing live"; (3) the debugging surface target is DIFFABLE not readable — agents grep structure; first-divergence paths and per-program-path subtree hashes beat human-prose logs; (4) counterfactual replay (edit the journal, re-run: the mock IS the recorded answer) falls out of journal-as-data and is priced as a headline demo; (5) caveats priced up front: journal growth/GC tombstones, PII capture, prim contract versioning. v1 chapters: grants, journal, replay, call-sites, acceptance.|) *)
